\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 6: Preference, utility, and choice when alternatives are uncertain
 prospects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Need to complete: Problem 5.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We consider agents in a world in which alternatives are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

uncertain prospects.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 An example is an agent who chooses between two lottery tickets that give
 him/her different payments with different odds.
 Another example is an agent who decides, when leaving home in the morning,
 whether to carry an umbrella or not.
 Carrying the umbrella gives the agent an uncertain level of welfare.
 If it rains, the umbrella will come in handy and the benefit of not getting
 wet will compensate the effort of carrying it.
 If it does not rain, the umbrella will just occupy space in his/her bag
 without benefit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In contrast to the theory developed in the previous lectures, here it is
 not trivial what the set of alternatives is.
 In abstract choice theory alternatives are elements of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 In consumer theory, alternatives are bundles of goods, i.e., elements of
 some Euclidean space.
 What mathematical objects represent uncertain prospects? One has to solve
 this question before talking about choice and preference over this sort
 of alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let us remark that since abstract choice theory is general, then all results
 in Chapter 2 apply to choice and preference when alternatives are uncertain
 prospects.
 Parallel to consumer theory, our objective here is to impose more structure
 in the model, so we can better characterize the behavior of agents in these
 uncertain situations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
6.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The mathematical structure of an uncertain prospect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first uncertain prospects that we study are the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

risky prospects.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 We assume that there is a set of possbile outcomes that we denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

.
 This set is different from the set of alternatives.
 A 
\series bold
risky
\series default
 
\series bold
prospect
\series default
, or an alternative, is a lottery over the outcomes 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

, i.e., a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

probability measure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

distribution
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

.
 We denote the set of risky prospects over 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Risky prospects are a natural and handy representation for many uncertain
 prospects.
 These are all the prospects for which it is reasonable that the agent envisions
 some outcomes happening with some probabilities.
 For the moment, we will not inquire where these probabilities come from.
 We will just assume that they exist and that the agent accepts them as
 true.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The preceding two paragraphs seem to leave open the possibility that some
 uncertain prospects do not fit into the mathematical structure of a risky
 prospect.
 It is not trivial to think about one that does not.
 Nevertheless, not only are there some, but also they seem to play an important
 role in agents' behavior.
 A large part of the modern literature on decision theory has been motivated
 by the existence of these uncertain prospects that cannot be represented
 by risky prospects.
 We will come back to this issue later.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.1.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finite set of outcomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\equiv\{z_{1},\ldots,z_{n}\}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

.
 A probability measure or distribution on 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is a function 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi:Z\rightarrow[0,1]$
\end_inset

 such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in Z$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(z)\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{z\in Z}\pi(z)=1$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 is also known as a 
\emph on
simple lottery
\emph default
.
 The set of probability measures on 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 In order to simplify notation, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

, we write 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(z_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A risky prospect over a finite set of outcomes can be graphically represented
 by a rooted tree.
 Final nodes are assigned to outcomes.
 The probability of each outcome is the probability with which that final
 node in the tree is reached.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\equiv\{z_{1},z_{2}\}$
\end_inset

, one can graphically represent 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 by means of the segment 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Each point 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 represents the lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}=x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{2}=1-x$
\end_inset

.
 Conversely, each lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 is represented by the point 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Analogously, if 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\equiv\{z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}\}$
\end_inset

, one can graphically represent 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 by an equilateral triangle with height 1 and whose vertices are labeled
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 A point 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in the triangle represents the lottery for which 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the distance between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and the side that opposes vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the distance between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and the side that opposes vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{3}$
\end_inset

 is the distance between 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

 and the side that opposes 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{3}$
\end_inset

 (the summation of the distances to the sides from each point inside an
 equilateral triangle is a constant).
 Conversely, each lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 is represented by the point whose distances to the respective sides are
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{3}$
\end_inset

 (one should be able to prove that given an arbitrary 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

, there is such a point).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n},$
\end_inset

 but we can observe 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n-1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(112).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u:Z\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{\pi}u\equiv{\displaystyle \sum_{z\in Z}}u(z)\pi(z)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In a simple lottery, the outcomes that may result are certain.
 A more general variant of a lottery, known as a 
\emph on
compound
\emph default
 
\emph on
lottery
\emph default
, allows the outcomes of a lottery themselves to be simple lotteries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(113).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 be a list of non-negative numbers such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_{k}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\pi^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 be a list of elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
compound lottery of 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\pi^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 with weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha_{i}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is the element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}\pi^{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{K}\pi^{K}$
\end_inset

, defined as follows.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}\in Z$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha_{1}\pi^{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{K}\pi^{K}(z_{i})\equiv{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{K}}\alpha_{k}\pi^{k}(z_{i})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For any compound lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $(\pi^{1},\ldots,\pi^{K};\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{K})$
\end_inset

, we can calculate a corresponding 
\emph on
reduced lottery
\emph default
 as the simple lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=(\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{n})$
\end_inset

 that generates the same ultimate distribution over outcomes.
 The value of each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is obtained by multiplying the probability that each lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{k}$
\end_inset

 arises, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{k}$
\end_inset

, by the probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{k}(z_{i})$
\end_inset

 that outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 arises in lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{k}$
\end_inset

, and then adding over 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 That is, the probability of outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the reduced lottery is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi(z_{i})=\alpha_{1}\pi^{1}(z_{i})+\cdots+\alpha_{K}\pi^{K}(z_{i})
\]

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}\pi(z_{i})=\sum_{k}\alpha_{k}(\sum_{i}\pi^{k}(z_{i}))=\sum_{k}\alpha_{k}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

Therefore, the reduced lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 of any compound lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $(\pi^{1},\ldots,\pi^{K};\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{K})$
\end_inset

 can be obtained by vector addition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi=\alpha_{1}\pi^{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{K}\pi^{K}\in\Delta(Z)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.1.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Money lotteries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We also consider the case in which outcomes are real numbers, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\equiv\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 We interpret these outcomes as monetary values.
 We refer to the probability measures on 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 as money lotteries.
 Formally, an element in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 is a probability measure on the measure space 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbb{R},\mathcal{F})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{F}$
\end_inset

 is an appropriate sigma-algebra.
 In practice, we can make do by representing money lotteries by their cumulative
 distribution function (c.d.f.), i.e., the function that assigns to each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, the probability that the outcome turns out to be less than or equal to
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Prob}(z\leq x)$
\end_inset

.
 The generic c.d.f is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 A c.d.f.
 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 admits a denisty function, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Prob}(z\leq x)=F(x)={\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{x}}f(z)dz
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)$
\end_inset

 does not have to exist.
 The advantage of a formalism based on distribution functions over one based
 on density functions, however, is that the first is completely general.
 It does not exclude a priori the possibility of a discrete set of outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series default
For a continuous variable, there is an assignment of utility values 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

 to nonnegative amounts of money with the proerty that any 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 can be evaluated by a utility function of the following form:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(114).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset


\series default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{F}u\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}}u\ dF
\]

\end_inset

Whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

admits a density function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{F}u\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}}u(z)f(z)\ dz
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{F}u$
\end_inset

 is the mathematical expectation, over the realizations of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, of the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 The latter takes the place of the values 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})$
\end_inset

 used in the discrete treatment.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Given a distribution function 
\begin_inset Formula $F(x)$
\end_inset

, the expected value of a function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(x)$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\int\phi(x)dF(x)$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 has an associated density function 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

, this expression is exactly equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\int\phi(x)f(x)dx$
\end_inset

.
 Note also that for notational simplicity, we do not explicitly write the
 limits of integration when the integral is over the full range of possible
 realizations of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The strength of the expected utility representation is that it preserves
 the very useful expectation form while making the utility of monetary lotteries
 sensitive not only to the mean but also to other higher moments of the
 distribution of the monetary payoffs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is important to distinguish between the utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{F}u$
\end_inset

, defined on lotteries, and the utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 defined on sure amounts of money.
 For this reason, we call 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{F}u$
\end_inset

 the 
\emph on
von-Neumann-Morgenstern
\emph default
 (
\emph on
v.N-M
\emph default
) 
\emph on
expected utility function
\emph default
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 the 
\emph on
Bernoulli utility function
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(115).
 
\series default
\emph on
The 
\series bold
expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu_{F}\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}}z\ dF
\]

\end_inset

Whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 admits a density function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu_{F}\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}}zf(z)\ dz
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series default
Note that distribution functions preserve the linear structure of lotteries
 (as do density functions).
 For example, the final distribution of money, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, induced by a compound lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $(\pi^{1},\ldots,\pi^{K};\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{K})$
\end_inset

 is just the weighted average of the distributions induced by each of the
 lotteries that consitute it: 
\begin_inset Formula $F(x)=\sum_{k}\alpha_{k}F^{k}(x)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{k}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the distribution of the payoff under lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(116).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 be a list of non-negative numbers such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_{k}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{F^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 be a list of elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
compound lottery of 
\begin_inset Formula $\{F^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 with weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is the element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}F^{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{K}F^{K}$
\end_inset

, defined as follows.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in Z$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha_{1}F^{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{K}F^{K}(z)\equiv{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{K}}\alpha_{k}F^{k}(z)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
6.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Expected Utility Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Having developed a way to model risky alternatives, we now study the decision
 maker's preferences over them.
 The theoretical analysis to follow rest on a basic 
\emph on
consequentialist
\emph default
 premise: We assume that for any risky alternative, only the reduced lottery
 over final outcomes is of relevance to the decision maker.
 Whether the probabilities of various outcomes arise as a result of a simple
 lottery or of a more complex compound lottery has no significance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we follow the axiomatic method in the study of agents behavior.
 This method consists of two steps.
 The first is to state a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

short
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 list of axioms, or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

simple
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 principles, that one could expect are satisfied by agents' behavior.
 The second is to characterize the family of preferences that satisfy the
 axioms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the remainder of this section 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is a finite set (all results generalize to infinite 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 with an appropriate extension of the continuity axiom).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Expected Utility Theory, due to John von Neumann and Oscar Morgenstern,
 is an axiomatic theory that characterizes the preferences on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 that satisfy the following three axioms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom*

\series bold
\shape up
A1.
 
\series default
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete and transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It should be emphasized that, if anything, the rationality assumption is
 stronger here than in the theory of choice under certainty discussed in
 Chapter 2.
 The more complex the alternatives, the heavier the burden carried by the
 rationality postulates.
 In fact, their realism in an uncertainty context has been much debated.
 However, because we want to concentrate on the properties that are specific
 to uncertainty, we do not quesiton the rationality assumption further here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We next introduce two additional assumptions about the decision maker's
 preferences over lotteries.
 The most important and controversial is the 
\emph on
independence axiom
\emph default
.
 The first, however, is a continuity axiom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom*

\series bold
\emph on
A2.
 
\series default
\emph default
For each tripe 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\pi,\pi^{\prime},\pi^{\prime\prime}\}\subseteq\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 the sets
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
