\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The independence axiom is at the heart of the theory of choice under uncertainty.
 It is unlike anything encountered in the formal theory of preference-based
 choice discussed in Chapter 2 or its applications in Chapter 3.
 This is so precisely because it exploits, in a fundamental manner, the
 structure of uncertainty present in the model.
 In the theory of consumer demand, for example, there is no reason to believe
 that a consumer's preferences over various bundles of goods 1 and 2 should
 be independent of the quantities of the other goods that he will consumer.
 In the present context, however, it 
\emph on
is
\emph default
 natural to think that a decision maker's preference between two lotteries,
 say 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, should determine which of the two he prefers to have as part of a compound
 lottery 
\emph on
regardless
\emph default
 of the other possible outcome of this compound, say 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 This other outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

 should be irrelevant to his choice because, in contrast with the consumer
 context, he does not consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 together with 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

 but, rather, only 
\emph on
instead
\emph default
 of it (if 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

