Consider the case where there are only three outcomes.
 Suppose that we represent the indifference map in the simplex.
 Assume for simplicity, that we have a conventional map with one-dimensional
 indifference curves.
 We argue that as a consequence of the independence axiom, the indifference
 curves are straight, parallel lines.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Indifference curves are straight lines if, for every pair of lotteries 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\pi,\pi^{\prime}\}\subseteq\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi\sim\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\pi+(1-\alpha)\pi^{\prime}\sim\pi$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi\sim\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, but 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\pi+(1-\alpha)\pi^{\prime}\succ\pi$
\end_inset

.
 this is equivalent to saying that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\alpha\pi+(1-\alpha)\pi^{\prime}\succ\alpha\pi+(1-\alpha)\pi
\end{equation}

\end_inset

But since 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi\sim\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, the independence axiom implies that we must have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha\pi+(1-\alpha)\pi^{\prime}\sim\alpha\pi+(1-\alpha)\pi
\]

\end_inset

However, this contradicts expression (1).
 Thus, we must conclude that indifference curves are straight lines.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose for contradiciton that we have two straight but nonparallel indifference
 lines.
 A violation of the independence axiom can be constructed in this case.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\pi,\pi^{\prime},\pi^{\prime\prime}\}\subseteq\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Without loss of generality, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi\succsim\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha\pi+(1-\alpha)\pi^{\prime\prime}\succsim\alpha\pi^{\prime}+(1-\alpha)\pi^{\prime\prime}
\]

\end_inset

does not hold for lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 But this contradicts the independence axiom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Therefore, indifference curves must be parallel, straight lines if preferences
 satisfy the independence axiom.
\end_layout

