The 
\emph on
expected utility theorem
\emph default
 says that the decision maker's preferences over lotteries satisfy the continuit
y and independence axioms if and only if his preferences are representable
 by a utility function with the expected utility form.
 It is the most important result in the theory of choice under uncertainty.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
21.

\series default
 
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 satisfies A1, A2, A3 if and only if there is an index 
\begin_inset Formula $u:Z\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is represented by the function 
\begin_inset Formula $U:\Delta(Z)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 defined by: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \pi\in\Delta(Z):U(\pi)\equiv E_{\pi}u$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moreover, 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is unique up to a positive affine linear transformation, i.e., if there is
 another function 
\begin_inset Formula $v:Z\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is represented by the function 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{(\cdot\pi)}v$
\end_inset

, then there are real numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $a>0$
\end_inset

 and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in Z$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(z)=av(z)+b$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We prove both sides of the proof independently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

