 In the triangle, pick a point 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 At this point, we can construct three separate triangles within 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta DEF$
\end_inset

.
 The three triangles are: 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta DpE$
\end_inset

 with base 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}$
\end_inset

 and height 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{1}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta EpF$
\end_inset

 with base 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{2}$
\end_inset

 and height 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta FpD$
\end_inset

 with base 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{3}$
\end_inset

 and height 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 The area of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta DEF$
\end_inset

 is the sum of the areas of these three triangles
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=\frac{1}{2}b_{1}h_{1}+\frac{1}{2}b_{2}h_{2}+\frac{1}{2}b_{3}h_{3}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta DEF$
\end_inset

 is an equilateral triangle, then 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}=b_{2}=b_{3}=B$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
A & = & \frac{1}{2}B(h_{1}+h_{2}+h_{3})\\
A & = & \frac{1}{2}BH
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $(h_{1}+h_{2}+h_{3})=H$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the summation of the distances to the sides from each point
 in an equilateral triangle is a constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Let 
