\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Subjective Expected Utility Theory answers the fundamental question: when
 does an agent have a probability distribution over states and makes decisions
 using Expected Utility with respect to that probability measure?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The answer is axiomatic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
(22).

\series default
 A binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{F}$
\end_inset

 satisfies P1-P6 if and only if there is a convex-ranged proability measure
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 and a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:Z\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 non constant, such that the binary relation is represented by the funciton
 that associates with each 
\begin_inset Formula $f\in\mathcal{F}$
\end_inset

 the value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U(f)\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{\Omega}u(f(\omega))dp(\omega)}
\]

\end_inset

Moreover, if 
\begin_inset Formula $(q,v)$
\end_inset

 is another such representing pair, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p=q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is a positive affine linear transformation of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

